Day#10 – Hand Tracking in AR/VR

Introduction--
--Hand tracking in AR/VR is a controller-free input method that uses cameras, sensors, and machine learning to map skeletal joints, 
enabling natural user interactions like pinching, grabbing, and gesturing

How It Works--
--Sensor Input: Headset or external cameras capture infrared or visible light images of the hands.Neural Networks:
AI models process frames to segment pixels and identify hand locations.
--Skeleton Mapping: Software calculates 3D positions for knuckles, fingertips, and joints to mirror movements in virtual space.

Core Benefits and UsesNatural Interaction--
--Users interact with virtual objects using familiar, real-world muscle memory without holding physical devices.
--Direct Manipulation: Grab, push, tap, and rotate UI elements or 3D models smoothly using simple pinches or palm gestures.
--Immersive Engagement: Eliminating hardware controllers increases user attention and presence during training, gaming, and design reviews

Real World Example--

-- Meta Quest 3 uses built-in cameras and AI-based hand tracking to detect the position of the user's hands and fingers. 
This allows users to interact with menus, press virtual buttons, and grab virtual objects without using controllers.
-- Apple Vision Pro uses multiple cameras and advanced computer vision algorithms to track the user's hands and finger movements.
Users can select apps, scroll, and interact with virtual content using natural hand gestures.

